WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.340
Why is density greedy optimal

2
00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.980
for fractional knapsack? We will

3
00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.320
fill a bag with divisible

4
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:08.900
items, compute the exact profit,

5
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.340
and use an equal-weight exchange

6
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.980
to explain why the rule

7
00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:13.460
works.

8
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.620
Each item has a positive

9
00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.540
weight and a positive profit

10
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.020
for taking all of it.

11
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.980
Taking a fraction earns the

12
00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:22.660
same fraction of its profit,

13
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.000
so profit divided by weight

14
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.980
measures the value of each

15
00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:28.040
unit of capacity.

16
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.080
Our capacity is fifty, and

17
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.220
the items have weight-profit pairs

18
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:35.000
ten and sixty, twenty and

19
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.860
one hundred, and thirty and

20
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:38.740
one hundred twenty. Their densities

21
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.360
are six, five and four,

22
00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:42.020
so that is also the

23
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:43.480
descending order for filling the

24
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:43.760
bag.

25
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.420
Take the first item fully,

26
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.280
using ten units of weight

27
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.740
and earning sixty. Forty units

28
00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.000
of capacity remain, and no

29
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.800
other material gives more profit

30
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.900
per unit than the density-six

31
00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.260
item just selected.

32
00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.520
Take the second item fully,

33
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.080
adding twenty units of weight

34
00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.460
and one hundred profit. The

35
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:06.020
bag now has weight thirty

36
00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:08.020
and profit one hundred sixty,

37
00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.200
leaving twenty units for the

38
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:10.840
last item.

39
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.300
The last item weighs thirty,

40
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.120
but only twenty units fit.

41
00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.220
Because fractions are allowed, take

42
00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:18.560
two thirds of it, adding

43
00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.160
eighty profit and filling the

44
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:21.160
capacity exactly.

45
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.840
Total profit is sixty plus

46
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.780
one hundred plus eighty, which

47
00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.160
equals two hundred forty. This

48
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.880
total uses fifty units of

49
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.380
weight; taking the whole third

50
00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.040
item instead would violate the

51
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:33.820
capacity limit.

52
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.120
Why is that local density

53
00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.060
rule globally safe? Suppose some

54
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.780
feasible solution includes lower-density material

55
00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.600
while higher-density material is still

56
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:43.920
available.

57
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.120
Exchange an equal small amount

58
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.560
of weight from the lower-density

59
00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.060
material for the higher-density material.

60
00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.640
Weight stays unchanged, while profit

61
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.220
cannot decrease and strictly increases

62
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.440
when the densities differ.

63
00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.560
Continue exchanging until no such

64
00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.060
inversion remains. The resulting solution

65
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.560
takes the highest-density material first,

66
00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:06.200
which is the greedy structure,

67
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.420
so an optimal solution can

68
00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.080
be transformed into a greedy

69
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.940
one without losing profit.

70
00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.340
The ability to take equal

71
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.940
portions of different items is

72
00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.660
what makes that argument possible.

73
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.520
With indivisible items, the exchange

74
00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.180
might be forbidden, so the

75
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.640
same proof does not establish

76
00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.940
optimality for zero-one knapsack.

77
00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.720
Compute densities, sort them in

78
00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.080
descending order, and scan once,

79
00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:32.800
taking the smaller of remaining

80
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:35.580
capacity and item weight. Stop

81
00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:36.580
when the bag is full

82
00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.160
or the items run out;

83
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:40.080
forgetting the second condition can

84
00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:41.660
index beyond the list when

85
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.340
total available weight is too

86
00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:43.700
small.

87
00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:46.140
Sorting costs big O of

88
00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.960
n log n, followed by

89
00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:49.640
a linear scan, while an

90
00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:52.260
already density-sorted list needs only

91
00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.640
that scan. A conventional stored

92
00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:56.820
list uses linear space, and

93
00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.940
any additional sorting workspace depends

94
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:00.380
on the chosen sorting method.

95
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.060
Things to take note of:

96
00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.480
compute profit per weight, allow

97
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.040
proportional fractions, and state why

98
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.640
an equal-weight exchange preserves feasibility.

99
00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:11.980
Highest total profit and smallest

100
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:13.780
weight are different rules; the

101
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:15.200
density order wins because of

102
00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.440
this problem's assumptions, not because

103
00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.320
the word greedy sounds confident,

104
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.140
hmph.
